-- title:
--   A cube is contained in its circumscribed ball
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$, let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Then the cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ is contained in the closed Euclidean ball about $u$ of radius equal to the cube's circumradius:
--   $$A_h(u) \;\subseteq\; \overline{B}\!\left(u,\; \frac{h}{2}\sqrt{d}\,\right).$$
--
--   Equivalently, every point of the cube is at Euclidean distance at most $\tfrac{h}{2}\sqrt{d}$ from the centre; the factor $\sqrt{d}$ is the usual comparison between the maximum norm and the Euclidean norm on $\mathbb{R}^d$, and it is sharp, being attained at the corners. This is the estimate that converts a statement about tiles into a statement about balls, so that a covering by cubes of side $h$ can be traded for a covering by balls of radius $\tfrac{h}{2}\sqrt{d}$ and vice versa.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No sign hypothesis is placed on $h$. For $h \le 0$ the cube is empty and the inclusion holds vacuously, so the lemma is stated in the widest generality with no positivity assumption to discharge at the call site.
